 can take this prefix is called an       Mpfx ('prefixable morpheme') [GL 3.34 psm., 3.52].  A noun of the form       G + Mpfx is a "general prefixal compound"; according to context, it may       substitute for any 'specified prefixal compound' where the Mpfx is the       second constituent: e.g. G-P8 (N) 'small round object' could be inter      preted in various contexts as A-m8=P8 (N) 'hand-grenade', I-P8 (N)       'fruit', nZ\-P8 (N) 'bullet', n8-P8 (N) 'testicle', etc.  This prefix       may also be appended to loan syllables [e.g. G-po (N), G-bo (N)       [qq.v.]). Each entry with the G- prefix is also listed under the root       morpheme (the 'naked Mpfx') [qq.v.].  In most cases etymologies are       given under the Mpfx rather than under the prefixal compound./G-a(n)  (N)         LOAN           saddle     /< Tai (cf. Siamese \aan and Tai Nuea \aan1) ult. < Chin.    (cf. Mand.       1n); cf. Xixia \i [the correspondence of Xixia i to Lh. a is regular];       SYN. kB\ (N, Mpfx); cf. G-lQ(n) (N)/     I-mM=a  (N)                   horse saddle     G-a cY ve  (OV)               put on a saddle     G-a cY-c< ve  (OV)            ride on/with a saddle          /SYN. G-a k$-c< ve (OV)/G=Q-P7  (N)         LOAN           father's younger brother; junior paternal                                    uncle     /< Chinese    (Mand. sh7); this form is characteristic of the Lahu mV-nR       dialect./     G=Q-P7_ma  (N)                father's younger brother's wifeG-u  (N)                           egg     G-u u ve  (OV)                lay an egg     G-u=Pi    (N)                 egg yolk     G-u=phu   (N)                 egg whiteG=u-phZ  ~  G=o-phZ  (N)           1. older brother of a girl                                   2. a girl's mother's brother                                   3. wife's brother                                   4. man's brother-in-law   